"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,351\nEpisode 3\n\n3\n00:00:07,431 --> 00:00:09,532\nThe 10% myth\n\n4\n00:00:12,332 --> 00:00:14,857\nWe only use ten percent of our brains.\n\n5\n00:00:15,127 --> 00:00:16,595\nIt appears in films,\n\n6\n00:00:16,671 --> 00:00:18,738\nads and classroom posters.\n\n7\n00:00:19,078 --> 00:00:21,684\nImagine what we could do with the other ninety.\n\n8\n00:00:21,854 --> 00:00:24,162\nThe idea seems to come from a stretch.\n\n9\n00:00:24,589 --> 00:00:30,816\nThe psychologist William James wrote that people use only a small part of their mental resources.\n\n10\n00:00:31,235 --> 00:00:38,221\nIn 1936, a foreword to a bestselling book turned that into a falsely precise ten percent,\n\n11\n00:00:38,497 --> 00:00:39,930\ncredited to James.\n\n12\n00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:42,461\nBrain imaging tells a different story.\n\n13\n00:00:42,897 --> 00:00:44,333\nOver the course of a day,\n\n14\n00:00:44,497 --> 00:00:47,181\nactivity shows up across the whole brain,\n\n15\n00:00:47,409 --> 00:00:48,658\neven during sleep.\n\n16\n00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:52,744\nThere is no silent ninety percent waiting to be switched on.\n\n17\n00:00:52,914 --> 00:00:55,436\nBrain injury tells the same story.\n\n18\n00:00:55,820 --> 00:01:00,263\nThere is almost no area that can be damaged without some loss of ability.\n\n19\n00:01:00,724 --> 00:01:02,432\nIf ninety percent were spare,\n\n20\n00:01:02,592 --> 00:01:04,418\nmost injuries would do nothing.\n\n21\n00:01:04,588 --> 00:01:06,784\nAnd the brain is expensive to run.\n\n22\n00:01:07,181 --> 00:01:10,025\nIt uses a large share of the body's energy.\n\n23\n00:01:10,507 --> 00:01:14,091\nEvolution would not keep paying for tissue that sits idle.\n\n24\n00:01:14,261 --> 00:01:17,973\nThe grain of truth: not every neuron fires at once.\n\n25\n00:01:18,321 --> 00:01:21,301\nAt any moment, only some areas are working hard.\n\n26\n00:01:21,529 --> 00:01:23,742\nThat is efficiency, not waste.\n\n27\n00:01:23,912 --> 00:01:25,459\nWhy does it matter for teaching?\n\n28\n00:01:25,813 --> 00:01:28,004\nThe myth suggests a hidden reserve,\n\n29\n00:01:28,086 --> 00:01:29,405\nunlocked by a trick.\n\n30\n00:01:29,929 --> 00:01:35,276\nReal gains come from something slower: knowledge and skill built through practice.\n\n31\n00:01:35,446 --> 00:01:36,645\nDo this instead.\n\n32\n00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,784\nReplace the promise of hidden potential with a plan for practice.\n\n33\n00:01:41,097 --> 00:01:43,433\nShow students how much they have already built.\n\n34\n00:01:43,823 --> 00:01:47,313\nAnd be wary of products that promise to unlock the brain.\n\n35\n00:01:47,803 --> 00:01:54,483\nOne tip to share with students: there is no hidden ninety percent; practice is how ability grows.\n\n36\n00:01:54,653 --> 00:01:56,713\nYou already use your whole brain.\n\n37\n00:01:57,060 --> 00:01:59,366\nThe question is what you give it to work on.\n\n"